After she left, he slid the clock across the bench toward Mara. She stared at it. In three weeks he had let her sort screws, wind finished pieces, and sweep. He had never let her open anything.

"The case first," he said. "Then you tell me what you see. Not what you think. What you see."

Mara reached for the smallest screwdriver, and her hand, she noticed, was not quite steady.','The grandfather''s reply "It will be slow" most strongly suggests that he',NULL,'["is irritated that the woman interrupted him","is trying to hide how much he plans to charge","doubts that the clock can ever be repaired","values careful, patient work more than discussions of price"]',3,'By answering a question about cost with a statement about time, he reveals that the patience the repair demands matters more to him than money.',1,0);
