Mara reached for the smallest screwdriver, and her hand, she noticed, was not quite steady.','The comparison of the grandfather lifting the clock "the way one might lift a sleeping cat" primarily conveys his',NULL,'["indifference to the clock''s history","reluctance to accept the job","gentleness and care toward the object","playful attitude toward his customers"]',2,'Lifting something as one would a sleeping cat implies tenderness and caution, showing his careful regard for the clock.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-c78ff7c4cf74','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Every spring, mayors across the country pose with shovels beside freshly planted saplings, announcing goals of ten thousand or a million new trees. The photographs are pleasant, and the ambition is sincere. Yet the obsession with planting obscures a less glamorous truth: a young street tree is a liability before it becomes an asset. In its first three years, it needs regular watering, staking, and protection from mowers and road salt. Without that care, a significant share of newly planted trees in some cities die before they ever cast meaningful shade.

Mature trees, by contrast, deliver benefits that saplings cannot match for decades. A single large oak intercepts far more stormwater, cools far more pavement, and filters far more air than a dozen thin saplings planted in its place. Yet when a sidewalk needs repair or a utility line needs space, the old tree is often the first thing removed, because replacing it with a sapling counts as "no net loss" on a city''s spreadsheet.

Cities should therefore measure their urban forests not by how many trees they plant but by how much canopy they sustain. That shift would redirect money toward maintenance crews, watering programs, and engineering solutions that let sidewalks and roots coexist. It would make for duller ribbon cuttings. It would also make for cooler, greener streets.','Which statement best expresses the central claim of the passage?',NULL,'["Mayors should stop attending tree-planting ceremonies because they waste public money.","Sidewalk repairs are the main reason that urban trees die.","Cities should judge urban forests by the canopy they sustain rather than by the number of trees they plant.","Young street trees are more valuable than mature trees because they live longer."]',2,'The author''s concluding paragraph explicitly argues that cities should measure success "not by how many trees they plant but by how much canopy they sustain."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-6f018872b5a1','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Every spring, mayors across the country pose with shovels beside freshly planted saplings, announcing goals of ten thousand or a million new trees. The photographs are pleasant, and the ambition is sincere. Yet the obsession with planting obscures a less glamorous truth: a young street tree is a liability before it becomes an asset. In its first three years, it needs regular watering, staking, and protection from mowers and road salt. Without that care, a significant share of newly planted trees in some cities die before they ever cast meaningful shade.

Mature trees, by contrast, deliver benefits that saplings cannot match for decades. A single large oak intercepts far more stormwater, cools far more pavement, and filters far more air than a dozen thin saplings planted in its place. Yet when a sidewalk needs repair or a utility line needs space, the old tree is often the first thing removed, because replacing it with a sapling counts as "no net loss" on a city''s spreadsheet.

Cities should therefore measure their urban forests not by how many trees they plant but by how much canopy they sustain. That shift would redirect money toward maintenance crews, watering programs, and engineering solutions that let sidewalks and roots coexist. It would make for duller ribbon cuttings. It would also make for cooler, greener streets.','The author mentions the phrase "no net loss" on a city''s spreadsheet mainly in order to',NULL,'["suggest that utility companies deliberately mislead city officials","praise cities for carefully tracking every tree they plant","show how a flawed accounting method encourages the removal of valuable mature trees","explain why saplings are cheaper to maintain than mature trees"]',2,'The author argues that counting a sapling as equal to a mature oak hides a real loss of benefits, making removal of old trees seem harmless.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-82a8b3f3aaac','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Every spring, mayors across the country pose with shovels beside freshly planted saplings, announcing goals of ten thousand or a million new trees. The photographs are pleasant, and the ambition is sincere. Yet the obsession with planting obscures a less glamorous truth: a young street tree is a liability before it becomes an asset. In its first three years, it needs regular watering, staking, and protection from mowers and road salt. Without that care, a significant share of newly planted trees in some cities die before they ever cast meaningful shade.

