Cities should therefore measure their urban forests not by how many trees they plant but by how much canopy they sustain. That shift would redirect money toward maintenance crews, watering programs, and engineering solutions that let sidewalks and roots coexist. It would make for duller ribbon cuttings. It would also make for cooler, greener streets.','The sentence "It would make for duller ribbon cuttings" is best described as',NULL,'["an urgent warning that the proposal will fail","a bitter complaint about politicians who seek attention","a wry concession that the proposed approach lacks public spectacle","a nostalgic look back at past planting ceremonies"]',2,'The author dryly admits a minor drawback of the proposal before immediately countering it with the benefit of "cooler, greener streets."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-d9c261e1500a','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Every spring, mayors across the country pose with shovels beside freshly planted saplings, announcing goals of ten thousand or a million new trees. The photographs are pleasant, and the ambition is sincere. Yet the obsession with planting obscures a less glamorous truth: a young street tree is a liability before it becomes an asset. In its first three years, it needs regular watering, staking, and protection from mowers and road salt. Without that care, a significant share of newly planted trees in some cities die before they ever cast meaningful shade.

Mature trees, by contrast, deliver benefits that saplings cannot match for decades. A single large oak intercepts far more stormwater, cools far more pavement, and filters far more air than a dozen thin saplings planted in its place. Yet when a sidewalk needs repair or a utility line needs space, the old tree is often the first thing removed, because replacing it with a sapling counts as "no net loss" on a city''s spreadsheet.

Cities should therefore measure their urban forests not by how many trees they plant but by how much canopy they sustain. That shift would redirect money toward maintenance crews, watering programs, and engineering solutions that let sidewalks and roots coexist. It would make for duller ribbon cuttings. It would also make for cooler, greener streets.','Which finding, if true, would most strengthen the author''s argument?',NULL,'["Cities that funded tree maintenance gained more canopy cover over a decade than cities that funded only new plantings.","Some cities have planted more than a million trees since 2000.","Oak trees are among the species most commonly planted along city streets.","Most residents report enjoying community tree-planting events."]',0,'Evidence that maintenance funding produces more canopy than planting alone directly supports the author''s call to shift resources toward sustaining trees.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-2d7b2433dae2','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'To the Honorable Council of the Town of Ashford Mills,

Gentlemen,—I have this week completed the survey you commissioned regarding the proposed extension of the canal to the northern quarries, and I must, in candor, report that the undertaking is neither so simple nor so cheap as the petition laid before you has represented. The petitioners estimate the cost at eleven thousand dollars. I cannot, in good conscience, place it below nineteen.

The difficulty lies chiefly in the ground itself. Beyond the second mile, the soil gives way to a broken shale which will not hold water without lining, and the lining alone will consume a sum the petitioners have omitted entirely. Moreover, the rise toward the quarries requires not two locks, as they suppose, but four.

I do not write to discourage the enterprise. The quarries are rich, and a waterway would reduce the cost of hauling stone by more than half, to the lasting benefit of every merchant in the town. I write only so that the Council may decide with its eyes open rather than be persuaded by figures that will not survive the first season of digging. An honest estimate disappoints once; a dishonest one disappoints every quarter until the work is abandoned.

I remain, Gentlemen, your obedient servant,
Josiah Whitcombe, Engineer','What is Whitcombe''s main purpose in writing this letter?',NULL,'["to correct an unrealistically low cost estimate while still supporting the project if it is honestly funded","to persuade the Council to reject the canal extension entirely","to request payment for the survey he has completed","to accuse the petitioners of attempting to defraud the town"]',0,'He states that the petitioners'' figures are too low but adds, "I do not write to discourage the enterprise," showing he wants an informed decision rather than a rejection.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-27b89c14a67c','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'To the Honorable Council of the Town of Ashford Mills,

