I do not write to discourage the enterprise. The quarries are rich, and a waterway would reduce the cost of hauling stone by more than half, to the lasting benefit of every merchant in the town. I write only so that the Council may decide with its eyes open rather than be persuaded by figures that will not survive the first season of digging. An honest estimate disappoints once; a dishonest one disappoints every quarter until the work is abandoned.

I remain, Gentlemen, your obedient servant,
Josiah Whitcombe, Engineer','Which choice best describes Whitcombe''s view of the petitioners'' figures?',NULL,'["He considers them accurate but somewhat outdated.","He regards them as unreliable because they leave out major expenses.","He finds them too detailed to be useful to the Council.","He believes they were inflated to discourage the project."]',1,'He notes that the petitioners omitted the cost of lining "entirely" and miscounted the locks, so he sees their estimate as incomplete and too low.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-3a09fc59fff5','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'To the Honorable Council of the Town of Ashford Mills,

