I do not write to discourage the enterprise. The quarries are rich, and a waterway would reduce the cost of hauling stone by more than half, to the lasting benefit of every merchant in the town. I write only so that the Council may decide with its eyes open rather than be persuaded by figures that will not survive the first season of digging. An honest estimate disappoints once; a dishonest one disappoints every quarter until the work is abandoned.

I remain, Gentlemen, your obedient servant,
Josiah Whitcombe, Engineer','The sentence "An honest estimate disappoints once; a dishonest one disappoints every quarter until the work is abandoned" functions mainly to',NULL,'["shift the letter from a business matter to a personal anecdote","introduce new technical evidence about the soil near the quarries","sum up Whitcombe''s reasoning with a memorable contrast about the lasting cost of false figures","concede that the petitioners'' estimate may be correct after all"]',2,'The balanced, aphoristic contrast condenses his argument that an accurate estimate now prevents repeated disappointment later.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-e4a55199d6e8','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Lichens look like little more than crusty stains on rocks and bark, but they are among the most sensitive living instruments scientists possess. A lichen is not a single organism but a partnership: a fungus provides structure and shelter, while an alga or cyanobacterium supplies food through photosynthesis. Because lichens have no roots and no waxy outer layer, they absorb water and nutrients directly from the air—and, with them, whatever pollutants the air carries.

This vulnerability makes lichens useful. Species differ sharply in their tolerance for sulfur dioxide and nitrogen compounds. Shrubby, branching lichens tend to vanish first as pollution increases, while flat, crusty species persist in dirtier conditions. By surveying which forms grow on trees in a given area, researchers can estimate air quality without installing expensive monitoring equipment.

The method has limits. Lichens grow slowly, so a survey reflects conditions over years rather than days; a sudden spike in pollution may not register at all. Local factors such as humidity and bark acidity also influence which species thrive, so a sparse lichen community does not automatically indicate dirty air. Researchers therefore treat lichen surveys as a complement to instrument readings, not a replacement—an inexpensive way to map broad patterns and identify places where closer monitoring is warranted.','Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?',NULL,'["Lichens consist of a single organism that has adapted to polluted air.","Lichens are dangerous to trees because they absorb pollutants from the bark.","Lichens'' sensitivity to pollutants makes them useful, though imperfect, indicators of long-term air quality.","Lichen surveys have replaced expensive air-monitoring instruments in most cities."]',2,'The passage explains why lichens reveal air quality and then outlines the method''s limits, presenting lichens as a valuable but imperfect tool.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-7992c7ae9ad4','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Lichens look like little more than crusty stains on rocks and bark, but they are among the most sensitive living instruments scientists possess. A lichen is not a single organism but a partnership: a fungus provides structure and shelter, while an alga or cyanobacterium supplies food through photosynthesis. Because lichens have no roots and no waxy outer layer, they absorb water and nutrients directly from the air—and, with them, whatever pollutants the air carries.

