INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-9a5d332dc0ea','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Tidewater

Twice a day the river forgets which way it runs.
The salt comes shouldering up from the bay,
patient as a creditor, and the brown water
turns back on itself as if remembering
some errand it left undone upstream.

My grandmother said the marsh keeps two clocks:
one for the sun, one for the moon,
and that a person raised here learns to read both—
when to set the crab pots, when the mud
will hold a boot and when it will keep it.

I have stood on bridges in other cities
and watched the current slide beneath me, sure
and single, carrying its leaves away,
and felt almost sorry for it, never once
asked to turn around.

I live inland now, where rivers know their minds.
They hurry one direction, plain as a sentence.
But some nights, lying awake, I feel it still:
the slow pull backward, the long hesitation,
the water deciding, again, to come home.','The simile describing the salt water as "patient as a creditor" suggests that the tide',NULL,'["moves quickly and unpredictably through the river","returns steadily and inevitably, as if to collect what it is owed","is weak compared with the force of the river","is generous to the people who live along the marsh"]',1,'A creditor waits calmly but always comes back to collect, which mirrors the tide''s slow, certain return twice a day.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-41df3947754a','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Tidewater

