and watched the current slide beneath me, sure
and single, carrying its leaves away,
and felt almost sorry for it, never once
asked to turn around.

I live inland now, where rivers know their minds.
They hurry one direction, plain as a sentence.
But some nights, lying awake, I feel it still:
the slow pull backward, the long hesitation,
the water deciding, again, to come home.','In the third stanza, the speaker''s attitude toward the rivers in other cities is best described as',NULL,'["fear of their strong currents","envy of their speed and certainty","admiration for their beauty","mild pity for their single, unchanging direction"]',3,'The speaker says she "felt almost sorry" for a current "never once / asked to turn around," expressing gentle pity.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-b9e9882bd6a4','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Tidewater

