INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-2a0131ac9b22','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'With four hundred meters left, Dev was in fifth place, and fifth place did not qualify for anything.

He had run this race in his head a hundred times on the drive up, and in every version he had made his move at the final curve, sweeping past the leaders with a grace he had never actually possessed. Now the curve was coming, and his legs felt like borrowed equipment—heavy, unfamiliar, returned in worse condition than promised.

Ahead of him, the runner from Lakeview glanced over his shoulder. It was a small thing, a half-second turn of the head, but Coach Ferris had drilled it into them all season: a runner who looks back is a runner who is worried. Dev felt something shift in his chest, a door unlatching.

He did not sweep past anyone. He clawed. He passed the Lakeview runner on the inside with his elbows flailing and his mouth open like a gasping fish, and then he passed another, and then the finish line was simply there, closer than it had any right to be.

Third. He bent over, hands on his knees, and laughed—a cracked, breathless sound that surprised him. It looked nothing like the race in his head. It was better, he decided, because it was real.','As used in the passage, the word "clawed" suggests that Dev',NULL,'["moved cautiously to avoid a collision","broke the rules to gain an advantage","struggled forward with fierce, awkward effort","scratched an opponent as he passed"]',2,'Set against "He did not sweep past anyone," "clawed" describes a desperate, ungraceful push, reinforced by his flailing elbows.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-8e0a71fb19b9','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Popular history loves a solitary genius. We picture the inventor alone in a workshop at midnight, struck by inspiration that no one else could have summoned. The image flatters our sense of drama, but it distorts how discovery actually happens. Nearly every celebrated breakthrough was preceded by years of incremental work by people whose names have faded, and many were reached almost simultaneously by rivals working independently—a sign that the idea was, in a sense, ready to be found.

This matters for more than historical accuracy. When students absorb the myth of the lone genius, they learn to see talent as a lightning strike that either hits them or does not. Those who struggle conclude that they simply lack the spark, and they quit. Meanwhile, institutions that believe in lone geniuses pour resources into identifying a few stars rather than building the collaborative environments in which most good ideas actually emerge.

None of this means individuals do not matter; some people do see further or work harder than their peers. But we should tell the story of discovery as it usually unfolds: as a relay rather than a sprint, with each runner carrying the baton a little further before handing it on.','Which choice best describes the author''s point of view?',NULL,'["Individual talent is irrelevant to scientific and technical progress.","Historians should stop writing about famous inventors.","The myth of the lone genius misrepresents how discovery happens and has harmful effects.","Most breakthroughs result from sudden, unexplainable inspiration."]',2,'The author argues that the image "distorts how discovery actually happens" and discourages students and misdirects institutions.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-8e3ae6912a87','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Popular history loves a solitary genius. We picture the inventor alone in a workshop at midnight, struck by inspiration that no one else could have summoned. The image flatters our sense of drama, but it distorts how discovery actually happens. Nearly every celebrated breakthrough was preceded by years of incremental work by people whose names have faded, and many were reached almost simultaneously by rivals working independently—a sign that the idea was, in a sense, ready to be found.

This matters for more than historical accuracy. When students absorb the myth of the lone genius, they learn to see talent as a lightning strike that either hits them or does not. Those who struggle conclude that they simply lack the spark, and they quit. Meanwhile, institutions that believe in lone geniuses pour resources into identifying a few stars rather than building the collaborative environments in which most good ideas actually emerge.

None of this means individuals do not matter; some people do see further or work harder than their peers. But we should tell the story of discovery as it usually unfolds: as a relay rather than a sprint, with each runner carrying the baton a little further before handing it on.','The author refers to breakthroughs "reached almost simultaneously by rivals working independently" primarily to',NULL,'["argue that competition is the main driver of innovation","illustrate how difficult it is to determine who deserves credit","support the claim that discoveries depend on accumulated groundwork rather than singular inspiration","show that inventors often steal ideas from one another"]',2,'Simultaneous discoveries suggest an idea was "ready to be found," supporting the view that breakthroughs build on prior work.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-2f8e51fd316b','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Popular history loves a solitary genius. We picture the inventor alone in a workshop at midnight, struck by inspiration that no one else could have summoned. The image flatters our sense of drama, but it distorts how discovery actually happens. Nearly every celebrated breakthrough was preceded by years of incremental work by people whose names have faded, and many were reached almost simultaneously by rivals working independently—a sign that the idea was, in a sense, ready to be found.

This matters for more than historical accuracy. When students absorb the myth of the lone genius, they learn to see talent as a lightning strike that either hits them or does not. Those who struggle conclude that they simply lack the spark, and they quit. Meanwhile, institutions that believe in lone geniuses pour resources into identifying a few stars rather than building the collaborative environments in which most good ideas actually emerge.

None of this means individuals do not matter; some people do see further or work harder than their peers. But we should tell the story of discovery as it usually unfolds: as a relay rather than a sprint, with each runner carrying the baton a little further before handing it on.','The sentence "None of this means individuals do not matter" serves mainly to',NULL,'["introduce a new example of a famous inventor","reverse the author''s earlier position on lone geniuses","criticize students who lack ambition","qualify the argument by conceding that individual ability plays some role"]',3,'The author acknowledges that "some people do see further or work harder," limiting the argument before restating the main point.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-12bd32deaef6','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'For much of the twentieth century, sleep was regarded as a kind of shutdown—a period when the brain, like an idle engine, simply rested. Research over the past several decades has overturned that view. During sleep, and especially during the deep, slow-wave stages that dominate the early part of the night, the brain appears to replay patterns of activity first generated during waking experience.

In studies with rodents, scientists recorded neurons in the hippocampus, a region involved in forming memories, while animals explored a maze. Later, as the animals slept, the same neurons fired in the same sequences, often compressed into a fraction of the original time. Disrupting these replay events tended to impair the animals'' performance when they returned to the maze the next day.

Human studies point in a similar direction. Volunteers who learn a list of word pairs and then sleep typically recall more of them than volunteers who spend the same interval awake. Some experiments have even played faint sounds associated with specific learned material during deep sleep and found that the cued items were remembered better.

Researchers caution that much remains uncertain. Replay has been observed most clearly in animals, and the precise mechanisms linking it to human memory are still debated. Still, the evidence has decisively retired the idle-engine metaphor.','Which choice best summarizes the passage?',NULL,'["Rodent studies have proved exactly how human memories are stored during sleep.","Once considered a passive state, sleep is now understood to play an active role in memory, though important details remain uncertain.","Playing sounds during sleep is the most effective way for students to learn new material.","Sleep is a period when the brain shuts down so that it can recover energy."]',1,'The passage describes the overturning of the "idle engine" view and presents evidence for memory replay while noting that mechanisms are still debated.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-c06b94801f4d','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'For much of the twentieth century, sleep was regarded as a kind of shutdown—a period when the brain, like an idle engine, simply rested. Research over the past several decades has overturned that view. During sleep, and especially during the deep, slow-wave stages that dominate the early part of the night, the brain appears to replay patterns of activity first generated during waking experience.

In studies with rodents, scientists recorded neurons in the hippocampus, a region involved in forming memories, while animals explored a maze. Later, as the animals slept, the same neurons fired in the same sequences, often compressed into a fraction of the original time. Disrupting these replay events tended to impair the animals'' performance when they returned to the maze the next day.

Human studies point in a similar direction. Volunteers who learn a list of word pairs and then sleep typically recall more of them than volunteers who spend the same interval awake. Some experiments have even played faint sounds associated with specific learned material during deep sleep and found that the cued items were remembered better.

Researchers caution that much remains uncertain. Replay has been observed most clearly in animals, and the precise mechanisms linking it to human memory are still debated. Still, the evidence has decisively retired the idle-engine metaphor.','Which finding from the passage provides the most direct evidence that replay contributes to memory rather than merely occurring alongside it?',NULL,'["The hippocampus is a brain region involved in forming memories.","Hippocampal neurons fired in sequences compressed into a fraction of the original time.","Slow-wave sleep dominates the early part of the night.","Disrupting replay events impaired the animals'' later performance in the maze."]',3,'Interfering with replay and observing worse performance tests cause and effect, whereas the other findings only describe when or where replay occurs.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-4d20bd883df0','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'For much of the twentieth century, sleep was regarded as a kind of shutdown—a period when the brain, like an idle engine, simply rested. Research over the past several decades has overturned that view. During sleep, and especially during the deep, slow-wave stages that dominate the early part of the night, the brain appears to replay patterns of activity first generated during waking experience.

In studies with rodents, scientists recorded neurons in the hippocampus, a region involved in forming memories, while animals explored a maze. Later, as the animals slept, the same neurons fired in the same sequences, often compressed into a fraction of the original time. Disrupting these replay events tended to impair the animals'' performance when they returned to the maze the next day.

Human studies point in a similar direction. Volunteers who learn a list of word pairs and then sleep typically recall more of them than volunteers who spend the same interval awake. Some experiments have even played faint sounds associated with specific learned material during deep sleep and found that the cued items were remembered better.

Researchers caution that much remains uncertain. Replay has been observed most clearly in animals, and the precise mechanisms linking it to human memory are still debated. Still, the evidence has decisively retired the idle-engine metaphor.','The author''s attitude toward the research described is best characterized as',NULL,'["enthusiastic to the point of ignoring the evidence''s limitations","dismissive of animal studies as irrelevant to humans","doubtful that sleep has any connection to memory","confident about the overall shift in understanding but careful to note unresolved questions"]',3,'The author says the evidence "decisively retired" the old view while also stating that "much remains uncertain."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-48565dc0c97f','MAP','reading','mcq',5,NULL,'Read the sentence.

Her reply was so laconic that the reporters had to fill the remaining airtime with their own commentary.

What does laconic mean in this sentence?',NULL,'["cheerful and friendly","long and detailed","highly emotional","using very few words"]',3,'Reporters needed to fill extra airtime because the reply was very brief, so laconic means using few words.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-79bc65ac978a','MAP','reading','mcq',5,NULL,'Which pair of words has the same relationship as ephemeral : permanent?',NULL,'["fragile : delicate","brief : short","ancient : historic","scarce : abundant"]',3,'Ephemeral and permanent are antonyms, and scarce and abundant are the only pair of opposites.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-883eea974619','MAP','reading','mcq',4,NULL,'Which word best completes the sentence?

Despite the senator''s reputation for ______, she voted against her own party on three major bills this term.',NULL,'["generosity","independence","partisanship","eloquence"]',2,'"Despite" signals a contrast, and voting against one''s party contrasts only with a reputation for strict partisanship.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-40bc678fec22','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

The committee recommended that each applicant ______ a writing portfolio by March 1.',NULL,'["submits","submitted","will submit","submit"]',3,'After a verb of recommendation followed by "that," formal English uses the base-form subjunctive "submit."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-d369c5e2eea1','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

If I ______ in charge of the schedule, I would move the assembly to Friday.',NULL,'["would be","were","am","will be"]',1,'A hypothetical condition contrary to fact takes the subjunctive "were" in the if-clause.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-3275eff888ca','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence uses parallel structure correctly?',NULL,'["The internship taught her to draft memos, managing deadlines, and the importance of relationships.","The internship taught her to draft memos, manage deadlines, and build professional relationships.","The internship taught her how to draft memos, deadlines, and building professional relationships.","The internship taught her drafting memos, to manage deadlines, and building relationships."]',1,'All three items share the same infinitive form (to draft, manage, build), making the series parallel.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-1045f705d55c','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which choice best completes the sentence without creating a dangling modifier?

Having finished the experiment early, ______',NULL,'["the cleanup of the lab began before the bell rang.","the students cleaned the lab before the bell rang.","there was time to clean the lab before the bell rang.","the lab was cleaned by the students before the bell rang."]',1,'The opening phrase must modify whoever finished the experiment, and only "the students" names those people as the subject.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-2958f345f59c','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

Neither the coach nor the players ______ aware of the schedule change.',NULL,'["was","were","has been","is"]',1,'With "neither...nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, the plural "players," so "were" is correct.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-2ff348df60c2','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

The number of applicants to the engineering program ______ risen sharply over the past five years.',NULL,'["were","has","have","are"]',1,'"The number" is a singular subject, so it takes the singular verb "has."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-baa390672fea','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

Each of the proposals, along with its supporting documents, ______ to be submitted electronically.',NULL,'["need","needs","have needed","are needing"]',1,'The subject is the singular "Each," and the phrase "along with its supporting documents" does not make it plural.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-c49e564b28ea','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which sentence avoids an ambiguous pronoun reference?',NULL,'["When Maria spoke with Lena, she said that her project was late.","Maria told Lena that her project was late.","When Maria and Lena talked, she mentioned that her project was late.","Maria told Lena, \"My project is late.\""]',3,'Only the direct quotation makes clear that the late project belongs to Maria, the speaker.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-d2f4d3de90f8','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?',NULL,'["The museum closed early; however, the gift shop remained open.","The museum closed early, however, the gift shop remained open.","The museum closed early however; the gift shop remained open.","The museum closed early; however the gift shop, remained open."]',0,'A semicolon joins the two independent clauses, and a comma follows the conjunctive adverb "however."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-2ccd640e6dfb','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence uses a colon correctly?',NULL,'["The recipe: requires only flour, water, and salt.","The recipe requires only: three ingredients, flour, water, and salt.","The recipe requires only three ingredients: flour, water, and salt.","The recipe requires: flour, water, and salt."]',2,'A colon correctly follows a complete independent clause to introduce the list that explains it.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-a02d629580ea','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which sentence correctly punctuates the interrupting phrase?',NULL,'["The final speaker, a marine biologist who had spent a decade in Antarctica—held the audience''s attention.","The final speaker—a marine biologist who had spent a decade in Antarctica, held the audience''s attention.","The final speaker—a marine biologist who had spent a decade in Antarctica—held the audience''s attention.","The final speaker—a marine biologist, who had spent a decade in Antarctica held the audience''s attention."]',2,'An interrupting phrase must be set off by matching punctuation, so it needs a dash on both sides.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-45eead2b408f','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'The writer has only one brother. Which sentence is punctuated correctly?',NULL,'["My brother who lives in Denver is visiting next week.","My brother who lives in Denver, is visiting next week.","My brother, who lives in Denver, is visiting next week.","My brother, who lives in Denver is visiting next week."]',2,'Because the writer has only one brother, the clause is nonessential and must be enclosed by a pair of commas.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-a7b29f339d2a','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Only some of the school''s laptops were damaged in the flood. Which sentence conveys this meaning correctly?',NULL,'["The laptops, which were damaged in the flood, will be replaced.","The laptops that were damaged in the flood will be replaced.","The laptops which were damaged in the flood, will be replaced.","The laptops, that were damaged in the flood, will be replaced."]',1,'A restrictive "that" clause without commas identifies only the damaged laptops, while the comma version implies all were damaged.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-0d47aa4f9fbe','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which revision of the sentence is the most concise while keeping its meaning?

Due to the fact that the bus was late, we were late in arriving at the museum.',NULL,'["The bus was late, and due to this fact we were late in our arrival at the museum.","Due to the fact of the bus being late, we arrived late at the museum.","Because of the fact that the bus was late, our arrival at the museum was late.","Because the bus was late, we arrived at the museum late."]',3,'It replaces the wordy "Due to the fact that" with "Because" and "were late in arriving" with "arrived late" without losing meaning.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-c2cb2f65d1eb','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which transition best completes the passage?

The new policy was intended to reduce traffic downtown. ______, congestion increased by twelve percent in the first month.',NULL,'["Consequently","For instance","Similarly","However"]',3,'The rise in congestion contradicts the policy''s goal, so a contrasting transition such as "However" is needed.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-4ca1c5f9f9ed','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which choice most effectively combines the sentences?

The bridge was built in 1932. At the time, it was the longest suspension bridge in the state. It is now a protected landmark.',NULL,'["The bridge was built in 1932, it was the longest suspension bridge in the state at the time, it is now a protected landmark.","Built in 1932, the bridge was the state''s longest suspension bridge at the time and is now a protected landmark.","Being built in 1932 and the longest suspension bridge, a protected landmark is what the bridge now is.","Built in 1932, the state''s longest suspension bridge at the time, it is now a protected landmark and the bridge."]',1,'It correctly attaches "Built in 1932" to "the bridge" and joins the remaining ideas with a compound predicate, avoiding comma splices and awkward modifiers.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-50e392052051','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence is most appropriate for a formal research report?',NULL,'["The data suggest a modest correlation between screen time and reduced sleep duration.","The data kind of show that screens mess with how long people sleep.","Basically, it looks like screen time is pretty bad for sleep.","So, the numbers tell us screens are totally ruining everyone''s sleep."]',0,'It uses precise, objective vocabulary and avoids slang, filler words, and exaggeration.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-84eab88f120b','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

The candidate ______ the committee selected will begin work in May.',NULL,'["which","who","whose","whom"]',3,'The pronoun is the object of "selected" (the committee selected her), so the objective form "whom" is required.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-bb775e4dc68a','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which revision makes clear that the painting, not the artist, is nearly two centuries old?

The museum displayed a painting of a ship by a local artist that was nearly two centuries old.',NULL,'["The museum displayed a painting of a ship that was nearly two centuries old by a local artist.","Nearly two centuries old, the museum displayed a painting of a ship by a local artist.","The museum displayed a painting of a ship by a local artist, that was nearly two centuries old.","The museum displayed a nearly two-century-old painting of a ship by a local artist."]',3,'Placing the modifier directly before "painting" removes any doubt about what is old, while the other versions attach it to the artist, the ship, or the museum.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-2473c28e78ce','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

The collection of rare coins, which ______ been in the family for generations, is now on public display.',NULL,'["were","has","have","are"]',1,'The relative pronoun "which" refers to the singular "collection," so the verb must be the singular "has."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-4ac149bae061','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

Between you and ______, the proposal still needs significant revision.',NULL,'["I","mine","myself","me"]',3,'"Between" is a preposition, so it takes the objective pronoun "me."',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-8378a322e1a2','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence so that the comparison is logical?

The population of Lagos is larger than ______.',NULL,'["London is","the London","London","that of London"]',3,'A population must be compared with another population, and "that of London" supplies it.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-94ff591d48c3','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence uses correlative conjunctions correctly?',NULL,'["The report was both thorough as well as persuasive.","The report not only was thorough but persuasive also.","The report was either thorough or it was persuasive too.","The report was both thorough and persuasive."]',3,'"Both...and" correctly pairs two parallel adjectives, while the others mismatch the correlative pairs.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-beb045f23190','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which sentence correctly punctuates the list of cities?',NULL,'["The tour stops in Austin, Texas; Tulsa, Oklahoma; and Denver, Colorado.","The tour stops in: Austin, Texas; Tulsa, Oklahoma; and Denver, Colorado.","The tour stops in Austin; Texas, Tulsa; Oklahoma, and Denver; Colorado.","The tour stops in Austin, Texas, Tulsa, Oklahoma, and Denver, Colorado."]',0,'When list items already contain commas, semicolons separate the items to prevent confusion.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-f8d4d5759dfb','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

The company revised ______ policy after the employees voiced ______ concerns.',NULL,'["its, their","it''s, their","its, there","it''s, they''re"]',0,'The possessive "its" refers to the company, and the possessive "their" refers to the employees.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-814c32362038','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which choice correctly completes the sentence?

If the team ______ the warning signs earlier, it would have avoided the costly delay.',NULL,'["would have noticed","noticed","has noticed","had noticed"]',3,'A past contrary-to-fact condition uses the past perfect "had noticed" in the if-clause with "would have" in the main clause.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-f27d437762f2','MAP','reading','mcq',1,'Mara carried the small blue bucket down to the shore every morning. The tide had gone out, and shiny shells lay scattered across the wet sand. She filled her bucket with only the smoothest ones. By the time the sun was high, her arms were tired, but her bucket was full. She planned to paint each shell and give them to her grandmother as a gift.','What is the main idea of the passage?',NULL,'["Mara is afraid of the ocean.","Mara collects shells to make gifts for her grandmother.","Mara is looking for a lost bucket.","Mara wants to build a sandcastle."]',1,'Mara gathers shells she plans to paint for her grandmother.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-93ffdbbbd78d','MAP','reading','mcq',1,'Mara carried the small blue bucket down to the shore every morning. The tide had gone out, and shiny shells lay scattered across the wet sand. She filled her bucket with only the smoothest ones. By the time the sun was high, her arms were tired, but her bucket was full. She planned to paint each shell and give them to her grandmother as a gift.','Which shells does Mara put in her bucket?',NULL,'["Every shell she finds","Only the biggest ones","The ones that are already painted","Only the smoothest ones"]',3,'The passage says she filled her bucket with only the smoothest shells.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-32ae3dc8a0b3','MAP','reading','mcq',2,'Mara carried the small blue bucket down to the shore every morning. The tide had gone out, and shiny shells lay scattered across the wet sand. She filled her bucket with only the smoothest ones. By the time the sun was high, her arms were tired, but her bucket was full. She planned to paint each shell and give them to her grandmother as a gift.','Why were Mara''s arms tired by midday?',NULL,'["She had walked to her grandmother''s house.","She had been painting all morning.","She had carried a bucket full of shells.","She had been swimming all morning."]',2,'Her full bucket was heavy after hours of collecting.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-a0def9883762','MAP','reading','mcq',2,'Honey bees communicate through dancing. When a worker bee finds a good patch of flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a ''waggle dance.'' The angle of the dance shows other bees which direction to fly relative to the sun, and the length of the waggle tells how far away the flowers are. Scientists studied this behavior for decades before they understood it. Today, the waggle dance is considered one of the most remarkable examples of animal communication.','What is the main idea of the passage?',NULL,'["Bees build hives only near flowers.","Honey bees cannot find flowers without the sun.","Scientists dislike studying bees.","Honey bees use a dance to tell each other where food is."]',3,'The passage explains the waggle dance as bee communication about flowers.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-4ff02ded1910','MAP','reading','mcq',2,'Honey bees communicate through dancing. When a worker bee finds a good patch of flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a ''waggle dance.'' The angle of the dance shows other bees which direction to fly relative to the sun, and the length of the waggle tells how far away the flowers are. Scientists studied this behavior for decades before they understood it. Today, the waggle dance is considered one of the most remarkable examples of animal communication.','According to the passage, what does the length of the waggle tell other bees?',NULL,'["How many bees should come","Which direction the sun is","How far away the flowers are","What kind of flowers are there"]',2,'The text says the length of the waggle shows distance.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-65a46c32c954','MAP','reading','mcq',3,'Honey bees communicate through dancing. When a worker bee finds a good patch of flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a ''waggle dance.'' The angle of the dance shows other bees which direction to fly relative to the sun, and the length of the waggle tells how far away the flowers are. Scientists studied this behavior for decades before they understood it. Today, the waggle dance is considered one of the most remarkable examples of animal communication.','As used in the passage, ''remarkable'' most nearly means',NULL,'["worth noticing","easy to forget","ordinary","dangerous"]',0,'Calling it one of the best examples means it is worth noticing.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-22b1469e80f4','MAP','reading','mcq',3,'Honey bees communicate through dancing. When a worker bee finds a good patch of flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a ''waggle dance.'' The angle of the dance shows other bees which direction to fly relative to the sun, and the length of the waggle tells how far away the flowers are. Scientists studied this behavior for decades before they understood it. Today, the waggle dance is considered one of the most remarkable examples of animal communication.','Why did the author most likely write this passage?',NULL,'["To tell a story about a lost bee","To persuade readers to keep bees","To compare bees with ants","To explain how bees share information"]',3,'The passage is informational and explains the waggle dance.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-478740d1414f','MAP','reading','mcq',3,'The old oak stretches its arms to the sky,
Holding the clouds as they wander by.
Its roots, like stories, run deep in the ground,
Whispering secrets without a sound.','Which line from the poem contains a simile?',NULL,'["Its roots, like stories, run deep in the ground","Holding the clouds as they wander by","Whispering secrets without a sound","The old oak stretches its arms to the sky"]',0,'Only this line uses ''like'' to compare two unlike things.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-300b191c1657','MAP','reading','mcq',3,'The old oak stretches its arms to the sky,
Holding the clouds as they wander by.
Its roots, like stories, run deep in the ground,
Whispering secrets without a sound.','The phrase ''stretches its arms to the sky'' is an example of',NULL,'["onomatopoeia","personification","simile","hyperbole"]',1,'The tree is given a human body part and action.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-ee53abb0db07','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'The old oak stretches its arms to the sky,
Holding the clouds as they wander by.
Its roots, like stories, run deep in the ground,
Whispering secrets without a sound.','What does comparing the roots to stories suggest?',NULL,'["The tree has a long history hidden beneath the surface.","Stories are written on the leaves.","The tree is growing very quickly.","The tree is dangerous."]',0,'Deep roots like stories imply old, unseen history.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-712937603f57','MAP','reading','mcq',3,'In many cities, empty lots that once collected trash are being transformed into community gardens. Neighbors who had never met now plant tomatoes side by side. Beyond providing fresh vegetables, these gardens reduce the heat that pavement traps, give rainwater a place to soak into the soil, and create spaces where people gather. However, some gardens struggle because the land is borrowed, and owners may sell it with little warning.','Which statement best expresses the main idea?',NULL,'["Community gardens offer several benefits but can be unstable.","Empty lots should be sold quickly.","Tomatoes are the easiest vegetable to grow.","Pavement is the best surface for cities."]',0,'The passage lists benefits and then a challenge.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-4883c516dd32','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'In many cities, empty lots that once collected trash are being transformed into community gardens. Neighbors who had never met now plant tomatoes side by side. Beyond providing fresh vegetables, these gardens reduce the heat that pavement traps, give rainwater a place to soak into the soil, and create spaces where people gather. However, some gardens struggle because the land is borrowed, and owners may sell it with little warning.','The word ''However'' signals that the next sentence will',NULL,'["define a new term.","give another benefit.","present a problem that contrasts with the benefits.","describe the history of gardens."]',2,'''However'' marks a contrast with the preceding positives.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-1719c11bb75b','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'In many cities, empty lots that once collected trash are being transformed into community gardens. Neighbors who had never met now plant tomatoes side by side. Beyond providing fresh vegetables, these gardens reduce the heat that pavement traps, give rainwater a place to soak into the soil, and create spaces where people gather. However, some gardens struggle because the land is borrowed, and owners may sell it with little warning.','What can be inferred about a garden on borrowed land?',NULL,'["It will always grow more vegetables.","It does not need rainwater.","It is owned by the neighbors.","It might be lost if the owner decides to sell."]',3,'The owner may sell with little warning, so the garden is at risk.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-2f8aeb1e7ff2','MAP','reading','mcq',3,'Theo had rehearsed the speech forty times in his bedroom, yet when he stepped onto the stage his tongue felt like a lump of wet clay. The auditorium, usually echoing and empty, now hummed with a hundred murmuring voices. He glanced at his note cards, and the words swam like minnows. Then he spotted his sister in the third row, giving him an exaggerated thumbs-up, and a laugh escaped him. The knot in his chest loosened, and he began to speak.','What does the simile about Theo''s tongue suggest?',NULL,'["It was covered with dirt.","It was cold from the air.","He had been eating clay.","It felt heavy and hard to move because he was nervous."]',3,'Wet clay is heavy and sticky, showing stage fright.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-24f53185cf3f','MAP','reading','mcq',3,'Theo had rehearsed the speech forty times in his bedroom, yet when he stepped onto the stage his tongue felt like a lump of wet clay. The auditorium, usually echoing and empty, now hummed with a hundred murmuring voices. He glanced at his note cards, and the words swam like minnows. Then he spotted his sister in the third row, giving him an exaggerated thumbs-up, and a laugh escaped him. The knot in his chest loosened, and he began to speak.','As used in the passage, ''exaggerated'' most nearly means',NULL,'["overdone","quiet","mistaken","hidden"]',0,'A big, silly thumbs-up is overdone.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-62d969366cf7','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Theo had rehearsed the speech forty times in his bedroom, yet when he stepped onto the stage his tongue felt like a lump of wet clay. The auditorium, usually echoing and empty, now hummed with a hundred murmuring voices. He glanced at his note cards, and the words swam like minnows. Then he spotted his sister in the third row, giving him an exaggerated thumbs-up, and a laugh escaped him. The knot in his chest loosened, and he began to speak.','Why did the knot in Theo''s chest loosen?',NULL,'["His sister''s silly gesture made him laugh and relax.","He found his lost note cards.","He suddenly remembered his speech.","The audience left the room."]',0,'The thumbs-up made him laugh, easing his nerves.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-a572aa9bc66a','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Theo had rehearsed the speech forty times in his bedroom, yet when he stepped onto the stage his tongue felt like a lump of wet clay. The auditorium, usually echoing and empty, now hummed with a hundred murmuring voices. He glanced at his note cards, and the words swam like minnows. Then he spotted his sister in the third row, giving him an exaggerated thumbs-up, and a laugh escaped him. The knot in his chest loosened, and he began to speak.','How does Theo''s mood change in the passage?',NULL,'["From bored to excited","From confident to scared","From nervous to calmer","From angry to proud"]',2,'He starts tense and relaxes after laughing.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-af7f220026fc','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Long before refrigerators, people preserved food using methods that sound surprising today. Salting drew moisture out of meat, depriving bacteria of the water they need to multiply. Pickling immersed vegetables in acidic vinegar, creating an environment where most microbes cannot survive. Smoking coated foods in compounds that slowed spoilage while adding flavor. Although these techniques were born from necessity, many remain popular because people enjoy the tastes they produce.','What is the main idea of the passage?',NULL,'["Refrigerators were invented to replace salt.","Bacteria are helpful to food.","Vinegar is the best preservative.","People once used several methods to keep food from spoiling before refrigeration."]',3,'The passage describes salting, pickling, and smoking as old preservation methods.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-b212debfa434','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Long before refrigerators, people preserved food using methods that sound surprising today. Salting drew moisture out of meat, depriving bacteria of the water they need to multiply. Pickling immersed vegetables in acidic vinegar, creating an environment where most microbes cannot survive. Smoking coated foods in compounds that slowed spoilage while adding flavor. Although these techniques were born from necessity, many remain popular because people enjoy the tastes they produce.','According to the passage, why does salting help preserve meat?',NULL,'["It adds acid to the meat.","It coats the meat with smoke.","It removes moisture that bacteria need.","It freezes the meat."]',2,'Salt draws out water, depriving bacteria of what they need.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-faed5a297f9d','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Long before refrigerators, people preserved food using methods that sound surprising today. Salting drew moisture out of meat, depriving bacteria of the water they need to multiply. Pickling immersed vegetables in acidic vinegar, creating an environment where most microbes cannot survive. Smoking coated foods in compounds that slowed spoilage while adding flavor. Although these techniques were born from necessity, many remain popular because people enjoy the tastes they produce.','As used in the passage, ''depriving'' most nearly means',NULL,'["keeping from having","teaching","giving extra to","protecting"]',0,'Bacteria are kept from getting the water they need.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-7bc47fc463c6','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Long before refrigerators, people preserved food using methods that sound surprising today. Salting drew moisture out of meat, depriving bacteria of the water they need to multiply. Pickling immersed vegetables in acidic vinegar, creating an environment where most microbes cannot survive. Smoking coated foods in compounds that slowed spoilage while adding flavor. Although these techniques were born from necessity, many remain popular because people enjoy the tastes they produce.','What does the final sentence suggest about these old methods?',NULL,'["They were invented recently.","They are unsafe.","They are still valued for reasons beyond necessity.","They are no longer used."]',2,'They remain popular because people enjoy the tastes.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-b47e85354e4b','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'The lighthouse keeper''s log, which had once recorded every passing ship, now lay open to a page unmarked since spring. Ines ran her finger along the blank lines. Automated beacons had replaced her grandfather''s lamp years ago, yet the tower remained, stubbornly upright, like a sentry nobody had thought to relieve. She wondered whether it was loyalty or simple neglect that kept the town from tearing it down.','The comparison of the tower to a ''sentry nobody had thought to relieve'' suggests that the tower',NULL,'["keeps standing guard though its purpose has ended.","is about to be torn down.","is guarded by soldiers.","was recently built."]',0,'A sentry never relieved keeps his post after the need is gone.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-ceaad50fbfaf','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'The lighthouse keeper''s log, which had once recorded every passing ship, now lay open to a page unmarked since spring. Ines ran her finger along the blank lines. Automated beacons had replaced her grandfather''s lamp years ago, yet the tower remained, stubbornly upright, like a sentry nobody had thought to relieve. She wondered whether it was loyalty or simple neglect that kept the town from tearing it down.','Which word best describes Ines''s feelings?',NULL,'["bored","terrified","wistful","furious"]',2,'She gazes at the blank log and wonders about the tower''s fate, with quiet longing.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-df3f5e267155','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'The lighthouse keeper''s log, which had once recorded every passing ship, now lay open to a page unmarked since spring. Ines ran her finger along the blank lines. Automated beacons had replaced her grandfather''s lamp years ago, yet the tower remained, stubbornly upright, like a sentry nobody had thought to relieve. She wondered whether it was loyalty or simple neglect that kept the town from tearing it down.','What does the unmarked page in the log most likely symbolize?',NULL,'["A mistake by her grandfather","A coming storm","The end of the lighthouse''s active role","A secret message"]',2,'No ships are recorded because automation replaced the keeper.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-e1cf7095ad40','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Economists often describe a ''tragedy of the commons'': when many people share a limited resource, each has an incentive to take more than a fair portion, since the cost of overuse is spread among everyone while the benefit goes to the individual. A pasture shared by herders is the classic example. Critics point out, however, that many communities have managed shared resources sustainably through agreed-upon rules, suggesting the ''tragedy'' is not inevitable.','Which statement best summarizes the passage?',NULL,'["Shared resources may be overused, though communities can prevent this.","Economists disagree about what a pasture is.","Resources always run out eventually.","Herders should never share pastures."]',0,'It describes the problem and then a counterargument.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-6e5a14178744','MAP','reading','mcq',4,'Economists often describe a ''tragedy of the commons'': when many people share a limited resource, each has an incentive to take more than a fair portion, since the cost of overuse is spread among everyone while the benefit goes to the individual. A pasture shared by herders is the classic example. Critics point out, however, that many communities have managed shared resources sustainably through agreed-upon rules, suggesting the ''tragedy'' is not inevitable.','As used in the passage, ''incentive'' most nearly means',NULL,'["warning","motivation","punishment","agreement"]',1,'An incentive is a reason that motivates action.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-fc1f646737f0','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Economists often describe a ''tragedy of the commons'': when many people share a limited resource, each has an incentive to take more than a fair portion, since the cost of overuse is spread among everyone while the benefit goes to the individual. A pasture shared by herders is the classic example. Critics point out, however, that many communities have managed shared resources sustainably through agreed-upon rules, suggesting the ''tragedy'' is not inevitable.','Why does each person have an incentive to overuse a shared resource?',NULL,'["Herders do not own their animals.","Shared resources have no value.","Everyone dislikes sharing.","The individual gets the full benefit while the cost is shared."]',3,'The passage states the benefit is individual and the cost is spread out.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-efab05bcb51d','MAP','reading','mcq',5,'Economists often describe a ''tragedy of the commons'': when many people share a limited resource, each has an incentive to take more than a fair portion, since the cost of overuse is spread among everyone while the benefit goes to the individual. A pasture shared by herders is the classic example. Critics point out, however, that many communities have managed shared resources sustainably through agreed-upon rules, suggesting the ''tragedy'' is not inevitable.','How does the final sentence relate to the rest of the passage?',NULL,'["It gives another example of a herder.","It restates the first sentence.","It offers a counterpoint to the idea that the tragedy is inevitable.","It defines the word ''commons''."]',2,'Critics argue agreed-upon rules can prevent the tragedy.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-7ea4d662ab67','MAP','reading','mcq',1,'On Saturday, Diego''s dog Pepper dug a hole under the fence and escaped. Diego searched the park, the pond, and the baker''s alley. At last he heard a happy bark from the community pool, where Pepper was splashing in the shallow end, soaked and proud. ''You silly dog,'' Diego sighed, trying not to smile.','Where did Diego finally find Pepper?',NULL,'["In the baker''s alley","At the community pool","In the park","At the pond"]',1,'Pepper was splashing in the pool''s shallow end.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-b905f43ebbb6','MAP','reading','mcq',2,'On Saturday, Diego''s dog Pepper dug a hole under the fence and escaped. Diego searched the park, the pond, and the baker''s alley. At last he heard a happy bark from the community pool, where Pepper was splashing in the shallow end, soaked and proud. ''You silly dog,'' Diego sighed, trying not to smile.','How does Diego most likely feel at the end?',NULL,'["Furious","Relieved and amused","Bored","Sad"]',1,'He sighs and tries not to smile, showing relief and amusement.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-ee6f67f9d5c5','MAP','reading','mcq',2,'On Saturday, Diego''s dog Pepper dug a hole under the fence and escaped. Diego searched the park, the pond, and the baker''s alley. At last he heard a happy bark from the community pool, where Pepper was splashing in the shallow end, soaked and proud. ''You silly dog,'' Diego sighed, trying not to smile.','As used in the passage, ''shallow'' describes the end of the pool as',NULL,'["very cold","not deep","crowded","noisy"]',1,'Shallow means not deep.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-477fbf5c2d46','MAP','reading','mcq',3,NULL,'Which word means almost the same as ''reluctant''?',NULL,'["generous","careless","unwilling","eager"]',2,'Reluctant means unwilling or hesitant.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-914b44e929f6','MAP','reading','mcq',2,NULL,'Which word means almost the same as ''abundant''?',NULL,'["rare","tiny","plentiful","fragile"]',2,'Abundant means existing in large amounts.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-6b1de1af9498','MAP','reading','mcq',1,NULL,'Which word is the opposite of ''ancient''?',NULL,'["old","modern","dusty","famous"]',1,'Ancient means very old; modern is its opposite.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-06aea3f48572','MAP','reading','mcq',1,NULL,'Glove is to hand as sock is to ____.',NULL,'["shoe","leg","foot","finger"]',2,'A glove covers a hand; a sock covers a foot.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-c914f5c98ac4','MAP','reading','mcq',4,NULL,'The detective''s ____ observation, noticing even a single loose thread, solved the case. Which word best completes the sentence?',NULL,'["careless","hasty","vague","meticulous"]',3,'Noticing tiny details shows careful, precise attention.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-reading-bf5c36393525','MAP','reading','mcq',5,NULL,'Which word best describes something that lasts only a very short time?',NULL,'["ornate","robust","perpetual","ephemeral"]',3,'Ephemeral means short-lived.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-a1f92940ace1','MAP','language','mcq',1,NULL,'Which word needs a capital letter in this sentence? My cousin lives in denver.',NULL,'["cousin","in","lives","denver"]',3,'Denver is the name of a city, so it is capitalized.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-53df678c0346','MAP','language','mcq',1,NULL,'Choose the correct word: The dog wagged ____ tail.',NULL,'["it''s","its","is","its''"]',1,'''Its'' is the possessive form; ''it''s'' means ''it is''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-62bf460bfd82','MAP','language','mcq',1,NULL,'Which sentence has correct end punctuation?',NULL,'["Where did you put my backpack,","Where did you put my backpack?","Where did you put my backpack:","Where did you put my backpack."]',1,'A direct question ends with a question mark.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-9edf2bc3022e','MAP','language','mcq',1,NULL,'Choose the correct word: Tom and Sam ____ to the store yesterday.',NULL,'["gone","goed","going","went"]',3,'''Went'' is the past tense of ''go''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-b415d2fd5002','MAP','language','mcq',2,NULL,'Which sentence uses commas correctly in a series?',NULL,'["We bought, apples bananas and grapes.","We bought apples, bananas and, grapes.","We bought apples, bananas, and grapes.","We bought apples bananas, and, grapes."]',2,'Items in a series are separated by commas.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-4795b519d964','MAP','language','mcq',2,NULL,'Choose the correct word: The students left ____ jackets in the gym.',NULL,'["their","there","theirs","they''re"]',0,'''Their'' shows possession.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-e327c7cdf547','MAP','language','mcq',2,NULL,'Choose the correct verb: Each of the players ____ a uniform.',NULL,'["are having","has","having","have"]',1,'''Each'' is singular, so it takes ''has''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-b949398164bd','MAP','language','mcq',2,NULL,'Which is the correct possessive? The ____ toys were scattered across the floor.',NULL,'["childrens''","childrens","childrens''s","children''s"]',3,'Children is already plural, so add apostrophe plus s.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-7c4778615005','MAP','language','mcq',2,NULL,'Choose the correct word: My sister is taller ____ I am.',NULL,'["thin","that","then","than"]',3,'''Than'' is used in comparisons.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-ed043de97707','MAP','language','mcq',2,NULL,'Choose the correct word: ____ jacket is left on the bench?',NULL,'["Who''s","Whose","Who","Whos"]',1,'''Whose'' asks about possession; ''who''s'' means ''who is''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-36319f101e2e','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Which choice correctly fixes this run-on? The bus was late we waited in the cold.',NULL,'["The bus was late, we waited in the cold.","The bus was late, so we waited in the cold.","The bus was late we, waited in the cold.","The bus, was late we waited in the cold."]',1,'A comma plus a conjunction joins two independent clauses.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-ae3b8526d889','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Choose the correct word: When Maria and Ana arrived, ____ coats were wet.',NULL,'["its","his","their","her"]',2,'Two people require the plural possessive ''their''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-ce099b068b01','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Choose the correct word: The girl ____ won the race is my cousin.',NULL,'["who","whose","whom","which"]',0,'''Who'' is the subject of ''won'' and refers to a person.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-4916fb280e8d','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Choose the word that keeps the list parallel: Lena likes swimming, hiking, and ____.',NULL,'["bike","biking","to bike","she bikes"]',1,'All items should be -ing forms.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-bd3c47411329','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Choose the correct verb: Yesterday, Jo opened the door and ____ inside.',NULL,'["is stepping","steps","stepped","will step"]',2,'Both verbs must be in the past tense.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-85a643348515','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Choose the correct word: The weather will ____ our plans.',NULL,'["effects","effect","affection","affect"]',3,'''Affect'' is the verb meaning to influence.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-bc94ac58eb32','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Which sentence uses a comma correctly after an introductory clause?',NULL,'["When the bell rang, the students lined up.","When, the bell rang the students lined up.","When the bell rang the students, lined up.","When the bell rang the students lined up,"]',0,'A comma follows an introductory dependent clause.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-73d26a897a12','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Which word needs a capital letter in this sentence? My uncle drove to lake Michigan on Monday.',NULL,'["uncle","lake","drove","on"]',1,'Lake Michigan is a proper name, so both words are capitalized.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-bc59241f3e23','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Choose the correct verb: The collection of rare coins ____ displayed in the museum.',NULL,'["is","were","are","have been"]',0,'The subject ''collection'' is singular.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-07fdd61b41c3','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Which sentence is in active voice?',NULL,'["The baseball broke the window.","The window was broken by the baseball.","The window has been broken by a baseball.","Broken was the window by the baseball."]',0,'The subject performs the action in active voice.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-ff494123de01','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Which group of words is NOT a complete sentence?',NULL,'["Practice makes perfect.","Although the team practiced all week.","The team practiced all week.","Did the team practice?"]',1,'It begins with a subordinating word and leaves the thought unfinished.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-6fb5f33cb6a4','MAP','language','mcq',3,NULL,'Choose the correct word: Honesty is a core ____ of our school.',NULL,'["principals","principle","principel","principal"]',1,'''Principle'' means a basic rule or belief.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-3f1199d74dfb','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence best combines these ideas? The museum opened early. It was crowded. Many families came.',NULL,'["The museum opened early it was crowded many families came.","The museum opened early and crowded and families many.","The museum, which opened early, was crowded with families.","The museum opened early, it was crowded, many families came."]',2,'It is clear, grammatical, and concise.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-31450be2d917','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence is the most concise without losing meaning?',NULL,'["The meeting is going to be beginning at noon time.","The meeting begins at noon.","The meeting, which is one that begins, begins at noon.","The meeting begins at twelve noon exactly at that time."]',1,'The other choices are wordy or redundant.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-43af50727e29','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Choose the correct word: The cat likes to ____ in the sun.',NULL,'["lie","lay","lain","laid"]',0,'''Lie'' means to recline; ''lay'' needs an object.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-55ebd7f26218','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Choose the parallel ending: The coach told us to stretch, to drink water, and ____.',NULL,'["rest is needed","to rest before the game","resting","we should rest"]',1,'The series uses ''to'' plus a verb.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-b46ede5445e7','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Which sentence has the clearest meaning?',NULL,'["Sandwiches were served to the guests who were on paper plates.","On paper plates, the guests were served to sandwiches.","She served sandwiches to the guests on paper plates.","She served the guests sandwiches on paper plates."]',3,'The modifier ''on paper plates'' sits next to ''sandwiches'', so the guests are not on plates.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-a5ecc24ffe09','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Several students share lockers, and the lockers belong to all of them. Which is correct?',NULL,'["The students lockers were freshly painted.","The student''s lockers were freshly painted.","The students'' lockers were freshly painted.","The students''s lockers were freshly painted."]',2,'A plural possessive ending in s takes only an apostrophe.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-47674172552d','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Choose the word that keeps the sentence parallel: The new park is both beautiful and ____.',NULL,'["useful","usefulness","it is useful","of some use"]',0,'''Beautiful and useful'' pairs two adjectives.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-8c2f2f8c917b','MAP','language','mcq',4,NULL,'Choose the best transition: Marcus practiced daily. ____, he won no medals this year.',NULL,'["For example","Therefore","Nevertheless","Similarly"]',2,'The sentence shows contrast with expectations.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-f69b88d7e4a3','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which sentence has a clear, correctly modified subject?',NULL,'["Having walked to school, the rain soaked the sidewalk.","Walking to school, Ben felt the rain start to fall.","Walking to school, the rain fell on Ben''s head.","Walking to school, the rain started to fall."]',1,'The introductory phrase must describe Ben, not the rain.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-3a4028c33919','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which sentence has a clear pronoun reference?',NULL,'["Sara told Lily that she had left her book at home.","When Sara and Lily met, she said she''d forgotten it.","Sara told Lily she left it there.","Sara said to Lily, \"I left my book at home.\""]',3,'The quotation removes the ambiguity about who ''she'' is.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-62e1364ec0ac','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Choose the correct form: If I ____ you, I would study more.',NULL,'["am","be","was","were"]',3,'Contrary-to-fact statements use the subjunctive ''were''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-a83876a5d351','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which sentence introduces its list with correct punctuation?',NULL,'["You will: need three items, a pencil, an eraser, and a ruler.","You will need three items: a pencil, an eraser, and a ruler.","You: will need three items, a pencil, an eraser and a ruler.","You will need three items a pencil: an eraser, and a ruler."]',1,'A colon follows a complete clause before a list.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-47625b2a9a31','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Choose the correct pronoun: Between you and ____, the test was hard.',NULL,'["myself","I","mine","me"]',3,'The object of a preposition takes the objective case.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-map-language-48d895157b5f','MAP','language','mcq',5,NULL,'Which sentence punctuates the appositive correctly?',NULL,'["My neighbor, Mr. Kim is a pilot.","My neighbor, Mr. Kim, is a pilot.","My, neighbor Mr. Kim is a pilot.","My neighbor Mr. Kim, is a pilot."]',1,'A nonessential appositive is set off by commas on both sides.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-183c38068f39','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'The hikers were ____ after the long climb, so they sat down and rested for an hour.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["generous","exhausted","energetic","curious"]',1,'The hikers needed rest, so they must have been worn out.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-61dad49321b8','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'Maya''s garden was so ____ that neighbors often stopped to admire the bright flowers.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["beautiful","careless","noisy","hidden"]',0,'Neighbors admiring the flowers indicates the garden was lovely.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-9667210c7f28','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Although the committee''s report was lengthy, its main argument was ____: the town needed a new bridge.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["ambiguous","contradictory","irrelevant","straightforward"]',3,'The contrast with ''lengthy'' and the clear one-line argument call for ''straightforward''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-9f15f6b9678a','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Botanist Ines Varga''s field notes are ____; she recorded the exact height, soil, and bloom date of every plant she surveyed.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["meticulous","sparse","speculative","hasty"]',0,'Recording exact details for every plant shows extreme care.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-cf48666ed055','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'The novelist''s early work was largely ignored, but critics later ____ it as a forerunner of the movement.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["obscured","postponed","acknowledged","forgot"]',2,'The ''but'' signals a shift from being ignored to being recognized.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-fb21a2e55d63','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Because the ancient manuscript had been ____ by moisture, scholars could read only fragments of its text.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["illuminated","preserved","translated","degraded"]',3,'Moisture damage explains why only fragments are readable.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-dd4e4aacf9f1','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The mayor''s response to the petition was ____, neither promising action nor rejecting the request outright.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["enthusiastic","decisive","hostile","noncommittal"]',3,'Neither promising nor rejecting describes a noncommittal reply.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-c131f624eb40','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Researchers found that the lizard''s coloring serves as camouflage, making the animal nearly ____ against the desert rocks.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["conspicuous","indistinguishable","unfamiliar","aggressive"]',1,'Camouflage makes the lizard hard to tell apart from its background.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-31d386a0b220','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The historian cautioned that the diary, while vivid, offers only a ____ view of the period, since its author lived in a single village and never traveled.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["balanced","comprehensive","limited","panoramic"]',2,'One village and no travel means the view is narrow.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-1c7c94a3bdac','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The sculptor''s style is anything but ____: each of her pieces departs sharply from the last, and no two look as though the same artist made them.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["expressive","unpredictable","innovative","uniform"]',3,'Pieces that differ sharply are the opposite of uniform.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-a7ca31bb9d32','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'The physicist''s lecture was ____ in the best sense: it distilled decades of dense mathematics into a handful of images that a general audience could grasp.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["tedious","lucid","austere","ponderous"]',1,'Making dense material easy to grasp is clarity, or lucidity.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-537db3ce3c1b','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Given the ____ nature of the evidence, since each witness recalled the event differently, the panel declined to reach a firm conclusion.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["corroborating","abundant","conflicting","conclusive"]',2,'Witnesses who disagree produce conflicting evidence.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-33e5bfb4d26e','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The author''s tone in the memoir is ____; rather than lamenting her difficult childhood, she recounts it with gentle humor.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["solemn","wry","indignant","bitter"]',1,'Gentle humor about hardship is a wry tone.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-ea1aca6a8f52','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Wind turbines can be especially ____ in areas with steady breezes, producing far more power than in calm regions.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["unreliable","unnecessary","effective","dangerous"]',2,'Producing far more power describes effectiveness.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-fc64d9aee123','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Critics praised the translator''s ____ rendering of the poem, which preserved its rhythm without sacrificing the precise meaning of any line.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["faithful","abridged","loose","careless"]',0,'Preserving both rhythm and meaning is faithful translation.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-c6d4caad3d83','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Her argument, though ____ at first glance, rests on a subtle distinction that most readers overlook.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["confusing","revolutionary","unassuming","bombastic"]',2,'An argument that hides a subtle point appears unassuming at first.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-5c5c7ce02da2','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'The cat was ____ when the dog barked, and it jumped onto the counter.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["pleased","sleepy","startled","bored"]',2,'Jumping away suggests the cat was surprised.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-783ccf2fea1b','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'To ____ the spread of invasive plants, park workers removed seedlings by hand each spring.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["encourage","ignore","curb","celebrate"]',2,'Removing seedlings is meant to limit the plants'' spread.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-f16a4dbdd08a','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The treaty''s wording was deliberately ____ so that both nations could interpret its terms in the way that best suited them.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["forceful","lengthy","exact","vague"]',3,'Flexible interpretation requires imprecise wording.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-c602102ecebc','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Although the economist''s forecast seemed ____ to some colleagues, who saw no evidence for such rapid growth, subsequent data supported it.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["implausible","redundant","cautious","unsurprising"]',0,'Colleagues who saw no evidence would find it hard to believe.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-0f23a5207557','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The museum''s new exhibit ____ visitors to consider how everyday objects reflect the values of the people who made them.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["invites","excuses","warns","deceives"]',0,'An exhibit prompting reflection invites visitors to consider something.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-ed939c27ef73','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'The committee''s decision, far from being ____, followed a year of deliberation and public comment.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["measured","capricious","thorough","transparent"]',1,'A year of deliberation contradicts a whimsical, capricious choice.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-172b3007172e','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Sea otters are considered a ____ species because their presence helps keep kelp forests healthy.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["pivotal","redundant","fragile","negligible"]',0,'A species that sustains an ecosystem plays a pivotal role.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-207006cd76df','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The poet''s reputation, once ____, has grown steadily since her letters were published decades after her death.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["notorious","enviable","obscure","secure"]',2,'Growth since publication implies her reputation was previously little known.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-5e46520cae09','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'The engineer''s confidence was ____: she had tested the design under dozens of conditions before declaring it safe.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["fleeting","arbitrary","well founded","reckless"]',2,'Extensive testing justifies confidence.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-5f142c2ef981','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'The soup was so ____ that Leo had to wait for it to cool before eating.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',NULL,'["cold","hot","plain","sweet"]',1,'Waiting for it to cool means it was hot.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-aab26f4877eb','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'In 1894, a young lighthouse keeper named Abel Rourke began a journal. He recorded the weather, passing ships, and, increasingly, his loneliness. The journal, discovered in an attic last year, offers historians a rare personal account of coastal life.','Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?',NULL,'["To compare two journals from the same era.","To argue that lighthouse keepers were poorly treated.","To introduce a historical document and explain why it is valuable.","To describe how lighthouses operate."]',2,'The text presents the journal and notes its value to historians.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-11448dcbe91b','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Many people assume that bees die immediately after stinging. In fact, only honeybees have barbed stingers that lodge in skin; bumblebees and many other species can sting repeatedly. The misconception persists because honeybees are the species people most often encounter.','Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?',NULL,'["It corrects a common belief and then explains why the belief persists.","It presents a hypothesis and then describes an experiment that tests it.","It contrasts two scientific theories and then favors one.","It describes a problem and then proposes a solution."]',0,'The text refutes a misconception and then accounts for it.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-9081f0f59d8f','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Economist Dara Okafor argues that remote work has reshaped small-town economies. Cafes, hardware stores, and childcare centers have reported rising demand since 2020. Critics, however, note that these gains are concentrated in towns within a two-hour drive of a major city. Okafor concedes the point but maintains that even this limited effect is significant.','Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence?',NULL,'["It introduces a limitation on the claim presented earlier.","It provides a specific example supporting Okafor''s claim.","It defines a term used in the previous sentence.","It restates Okafor''s central argument in different terms."]',0,'The critics'' note qualifies how widely the claimed gains apply.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-9f239c63ce34','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'The kitchen smelled of cardamom and burnt sugar. Nadia stood at the counter, flour to her elbows, listening for her grandmother''s footsteps on the stairs. Each creak of the old boards made her heart quicken, though she could not have said whether from fear or hope.','Which choice best describes the overall focus of the text?',NULL,'["A grandmother''s disapproval of her granddaughter''s cooking.","A character''s detailed recipe for a family dessert.","A character''s mixed anticipation as she waits in a familiar setting.","A narrator''s regret about leaving a childhood home."]',2,'Nadia feels both fear and hope while waiting.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-9ae85ec4075b','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Archaeologists long interpreted the stone circles of the northern plateau as calendars, aligning each with solstice sunrises. A recent survey, however, found that only four of the thirty-one circles align with any solar event, a proportion no greater than chance would predict. The finding does not rule out ceremonial use, but it undermines the calendar hypothesis as a general explanation.','Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?',NULL,'["To explain how solstices were observed in antiquity.","To defend the calendar hypothesis against criticism.","To propose a new method for surveying ancient sites.","To present new evidence that weakens a long-held interpretation."]',3,'The survey results cast doubt on the calendar hypothesis.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-cd60ea47d460','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Text 1
Urban planner Lila Moreno argues that cities should replace parking lots with housing, since doing so would ease the housing shortage.

Text 2
Transportation analyst Greg Hale cautions that removing parking without improving public transit could strand residents who depend on cars.','Based on the texts, how would Hale most likely respond to Moreno''s argument?',NULL,'["By arguing that parking lots are more valuable than housing in every case.","By proposing that cities build more parking garages instead.","By denying that a housing shortage exists in cities.","By agreeing that parking could be replaced but insisting that transit improvements accompany the change."]',3,'Hale warns against removing parking without better transit, not against the idea itself.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-e43bd4d80980','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Text 1
Some linguists claim that children acquire grammar mainly through an innate capacity, noting that all children, regardless of upbringing, master complex rules by age five.

Text 2
Linguist Pei Chen found that children exposed to richer conversation at home mastered certain constructions months earlier than peers with less exposure, suggesting that input matters more than the Text 1 view allows.','Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?',NULL,'["It applies the view described in Text 1 to a different language.","It offers evidence that complicates the view described in Text 1.","It restates the view described in Text 1 with additional examples.","It explains the biological origin of the capacity described in Text 1."]',1,'Chen''s findings suggest input plays a larger role than the innate-capacity view admits.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-97bd5a4ccc25','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Few visitors to the gallery notice the small brass plaque beside Ito''s painting. Yet the plaque tells the story of its rescue: in 1962, a flood submerged the storage vault, and conservators spent three years restoring the canvas. The painting, in other words, survives not only because of Ito''s skill but also because of the patience of those who saved it.','Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?',NULL,'["To explain the techniques Ito used to paint the canvas.","To criticize the gallery for hiding the plaque.","To describe the causes of the 1962 flood.","To suggest that the painting''s survival reflects the work of conservators as well as its creator."]',3,'The final sentence credits both the artist and the conservators.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-3c45d9abb484','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'Tomas planted tomato seeds in early April. By late May, the seedlings were tall enough to move outside, and by August the plants were heavy with fruit. Tomas gave most of the tomatoes to his neighbors.','According to the text, what did Tomas do with most of the tomatoes?',NULL,'["He sold them at a market.","He canned them for winter.","He saved their seeds.","He gave them to his neighbors."]',3,'The final sentence states he gave most to neighbors.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-c174ce520146','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'Octopuses are known for their intelligence. In laboratory tests, they have opened jars, solved mazes, and even escaped from sealed tanks. Researchers say these abilities suggest that complex problem solving evolved in more than one branch of the animal kingdom.','Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',NULL,'["Octopuses display problem-solving abilities that interest researchers.","Jars are commonly found in ocean habitats.","Octopuses are difficult to keep in laboratory tanks.","Mazes are the best way to test animal intelligence."]',0,'The text centers on octopus intelligence and its significance.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-575f1a52bcfb','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'When the town library extended its hours in March, the number of visitors per week rose from about 400 to about 650. The library director noted that most of the new visitors arrived after 6 p.m.','Which choice is most strongly supported by the text?',NULL,'["Most visitors prefer the library to other town services.","The number of visitors will keep rising every month.","The extended hours made the library accessible to some people who could not previously visit.","The library''s collection expanded in March."]',2,'New evening visitors suggest later hours reached people who could not come earlier.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-b5b856d7681b','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Fourth-grade teacher Ms. Adeyemi asks students to keep a weather log for one month. Each day, they record the temperature, cloud cover, and wind direction, and at month''s end they graph the results.','According to the text, what do students do at the end of the month?',NULL,'["They graph the results of their observations.","They compare their logs with a meteorologist''s.","They predict the next month''s weather.","They present their logs to the principal."]',0,'The text says they graph the results at month''s end.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-6bfd8291fa2d','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Tardigrades survive extreme conditions by entering a dried-out state in which their metabolism nearly stops. In 2017, researchers identified a protein unique to tardigrades that appears to shield their DNA from radiation damage. When that protein was introduced into human cells in the lab, the cells showed less damage after exposure to X-rays.','Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',NULL,'["Tardigrades are the only animals that can survive without water.","Human cells are naturally more resistant to X-rays than tardigrade cells.","Researchers have developed a way for humans to survive extreme conditions.","A protein found in tardigrades may protect DNA, and it reduced radiation damage even in human cells grown in a lab."]',3,'The text describes the protein and its protective effect in human cells.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-7ca1d691cd36','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Historian Priya Anand notes that the village''s records show no births between 1846 and 1849, while burial entries doubled. She adds that the records resume normal patterns in 1850, with a sharp rise in baptisms.','Which choice is most strongly supported by the text?',NULL,'["The village''s record keeper retired in 1850.","Burial entries rose because the village grew larger.","The village likely experienced a crisis that affected its population in the late 1840s.","Baptisms were illegal before 1850."]',2,'No births, doubled burials, and a later rebound suggest a population crisis.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-bdb2facd80a8','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Ruth Calloway''s novel follows a fishing family. Early on, the narrator calls the harbor "a cradle rocking the town to sleep." Near the end, the narrator calls the same water "a hungry mouth waiting beyond the rocks." The family''s boat, the Marigold, appears in nearly every chapter.','A student claims that Calloway portrays the sea ambivalently. Which choice best supports the student''s claim?',NULL,'["The narrator describes the harbor only once.","The narrator calls the harbor a cradle early in the novel and a hungry mouth near the end.","The novel follows a fishing family.","The Marigold appears in nearly every chapter."]',1,'Contrasting images of the same water show conflicting attitudes.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-64cae55f6079','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Ecologist Hana Sato hypothesizes that urban birds sing at higher pitches because low-pitched songs are masked by traffic noise. To test this, she recorded great tits in quiet forests and in noisy city parks.','Which finding, if true, would most directly support Sato''s hypothesis?',NULL,'["Great tits in city parks sang at a higher average pitch than great tits in forests.","Great tits in both settings sang at the same pitch.","Great tits in forests sang more often than those in city parks.","Great tits in city parks were larger than those in forests."]',0,'A higher pitch in noisy places matches the hypothesis.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-be067fb53c9c','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'A school surveyed 200 students about how they travel to class. Of those students, 80 walked, 60 rode the bus, 40 were driven by family, and 20 biked.','Which choice most accurately describes data from the survey?',NULL,'["Biking was used by 20 percent of students.","Riding the bus was the most common method, used by 40 percent of students.","Being driven by family was used by half of students.","Walking was the most common method, used by 40 percent of students."]',3,'80 of 200 is 40 percent, the largest group.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-1376aef9b5d4','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'A librarian recorded the books checked out in one week: fiction, 310; nonfiction, 150; poetry, 40; graphic novels, 100. She wants to show that fiction made up more than half of all checkouts.','Which choice most effectively uses the data to support her claim?',NULL,'["Poetry accounted for the fewest checkouts, at 40.","Fiction accounted for 310 of the 600 books checked out, which is more than half.","Nonfiction and graphic novels together accounted for 250 checkouts.","Fiction accounted for 310 checkouts, more than any other category."]',1,'310 of 600 exceeds 50 percent, directly supporting the claim.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-dfc57f5d5e99','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Elena had practiced the cello piece for weeks, but when she stepped onto the stage, the audience''s silence made her hands tremble. She closed her eyes, remembered her teacher''s advice to breathe slowly, and began to play.','What does Elena do to steady herself?',NULL,'["She asks the audience for quiet.","She looks for her teacher in the front row.","She switches to a simpler piece.","She closes her eyes and recalls her teacher''s advice about breathing."]',3,'The text says she closed her eyes and remembered the breathing advice.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-c9aa199adc70','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Scholars once attributed the unsigned portrait to the workshop of a famous master, citing its confident brushwork. But infrared imaging has revealed underdrawings that differ markedly from the master''s documented habits, and the pigments include a blue unavailable in the region until a generation after his death.','Which choice is most strongly supported by the text?',NULL,'["The master''s brushwork was inconsistent throughout his career.","The portrait was probably not produced in the master''s workshop during his lifetime.","The portrait was painted by someone deliberately imitating the master.","Infrared imaging cannot be used on portraits."]',1,'Differing underdrawings and an anachronistic pigment argue against the attribution.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-8c842bbecb0c','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Urban beekeeping has grown quickly, but ecologists warn that it can backfire. Honeybees compete with native pollinators for limited flowers, and in cities with few green spaces, the added competition may reduce native bee populations. The researchers recommend planting more flowers before adding hives.','Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',NULL,'["Cities should ban beekeeping entirely.","Native bees prefer cities to rural areas.","Urban beekeeping may harm native pollinators unless enough flowering plants are available.","Honeybees are less effective pollinators than native bees."]',2,'The text warns of competition and recommends adding flowers first.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-15dd0af45660','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'Every morning, the baker at Corner Loaf arrives at 4 a.m. By 6 a.m., a line of customers is already waiting outside the door.','Which choice is most strongly supported by the text?',NULL,'["The customers bake their own bread.","Corner Loaf opens at 4 a.m.","Corner Loaf''s bread is popular with customers.","The baker dislikes early mornings."]',2,'An early line of customers suggests the bread is popular.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-7cb87a3d8482','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Volcanologist Sam Ortiz measures gas emissions at the crater''s rim before and after each eruption. Before the 2019 eruption, sulfur dioxide levels climbed for two weeks. Ortiz suggests that tracking such increases could help officials issue earlier warnings.','Which choice best states Ortiz''s suggestion?',NULL,'["Craters should be measured only after eruptions.","Sulfur dioxide is the only gas worth measuring.","Eruptions can be prevented by reducing gas emissions.","Rising sulfur dioxide levels may serve as an early warning of eruptions."]',3,'The final sentence proposes using gas increases for earlier warnings.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-5ce75c79d139','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Economists often treat price as the main signal of a good''s value, yet studies of secondhand markets suggest that provenance also shapes willingness to pay. In one experiment, identical mugs sold for nearly twice as much when buyers were told they had belonged to a celebrated poet. The results imply that value is partly a story buyers tell themselves.','Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',NULL,'["Economists disagree about how to measure prices.","Buyers'' valuation of goods can depend on narratives about an item as well as its price.","Secondhand markets are more efficient than new-goods markets.","Mugs that belonged to poets are more durable than other mugs."]',1,'The experiment shows a story about the item changing what buyers will pay.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-016a38de05d3','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'At a science fair, 5 projects were in botany, 8 in chemistry, 4 in physics, and 3 in astronomy. Judges awarded ribbons to 2 botany, 4 chemistry, 1 physics, and 1 astronomy projects.','Which category had the highest proportion of its projects awarded ribbons?',NULL,'["Physics","Chemistry","Astronomy","Botany"]',1,'Chemistry 4/8 is 50 percent, higher than botany 40, astronomy about 33, and physics 25.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-92736610c585','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'When Ansel returned to the village after thirty years, he found the mill silent and the millpond choked with reeds. He walked its length slowly, touching the splintered railing as though it might answer him.','Which choice best describes Ansel''s reaction?',NULL,'["He is eager to restart the mill himself.","He is irritated that the village ignored his return.","He is indifferent to the changes.","He is moved by the decline of a place that once mattered to him."]',3,'His slow walk and touching the railing show emotional attachment.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-3bb127be0bbe','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'The Greenfield Zoo opened a new reptile house last month. It houses twelve species, including a Komodo dragon that visitors can watch being fed on Saturdays.','According to the text, when can visitors watch the Komodo dragon being fed?',NULL,'["On Sundays","On Saturdays","During the opening ceremony","Every morning"]',1,'The text states the feeding is on Saturdays.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-2f459998f7c2','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Rising sea levels are forcing some coastal communities to consider relocating. In Marlow Bay, residents voted last year to move their school and clinic inland, a costly decision that planners say will save money over the next fifty years.','Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',NULL,'["Marlow Bay residents regret their vote.","Sea levels are rising faster than scientists predicted.","Most coastal communities have chosen to relocate.","A coastal community has chosen to relocate services inland as a long-term response to rising seas."]',3,'The text focuses on Marlow Bay''s relocation decision.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-20c50ed62a52','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Seeds of the fire poppy can lie dormant in soil for decades. They germinate only after exposure to chemicals found in smoke, which is why the plant appears in great numbers on hillsides just after wildfires.','Which choice is most strongly supported by the text?',NULL,'["The fire poppy''s germination is triggered by conditions that wildfires create.","Fire poppies cannot grow unless neighboring plants have been damaged.","Wildfires are started by chemicals in fire poppy seeds.","Fire poppy seeds die within a few years unless they are burned."]',0,'Smoke chemicals trigger germination, explaining post-fire abundance.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-d2e92bffb819','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Historian Leo Brandt argues that the 1870s railway boom transformed the town of Calder from a farming village into a trading hub. Census records show Calder''s population rising from 800 in 1870 to 3,400 in 1880, and the number of registered shops growing from 6 to 52.','Which choice provides the strongest evidence for Brandt''s claim that Calder became a trading hub?',NULL,'["The number of registered shops grew from 6 to 52.","A railway boom occurred in the 1870s.","Calder was once a farming village.","Calder''s population rose from 800 to 3,400."]',0,'Growth in shops most directly indicates commerce.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-5fe23f6c0370','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Though critics hailed the symphony''s premiere, composer Tobias Reyes withdrew it from performance after only two concerts, writing to a friend that the final movement "says nothing I wished to say." He rewrote that movement entirely over the next decade, leaving the first three untouched.','Which choice best describes Reyes''s attitude toward the symphony?',NULL,'["He doubted his ability to compose anything further.","He regarded all four movements as flawed.","He was content with the work but feared harsh critics.","He was dissatisfied with one part despite the work''s positive reception."]',3,'Only the final movement was rewritten, despite praise.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-4c21a5ae35c3','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'At the Lakeside Market, vendors set up stalls at dawn. By nine, shoppers crowd the aisles, and by noon, most produce has sold out.','According to the text, what is true by noon?',NULL,'["Vendors begin setting up stalls.","Shoppers crowd the aisles for the first time.","The market closes.","Most produce has been sold."]',3,'The text says most produce has sold out by noon.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-44f8785481ab','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'A survey of 150 residents asked which public improvement they preferred: 60 chose parks, 45 chose bike lanes, 30 chose libraries, and 15 chose lighting.','Which choice most accurately reflects the data?',NULL,'["Parks were chosen by 40 percent of respondents, more than any other improvement.","Lighting was chosen by 15 percent of respondents.","Parks were chosen by a majority of respondents.","Libraries were chosen by 35 percent of respondents."]',0,'60 of 150 is 40 percent, the largest share but not a majority.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-60f5be01ee36','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'Regular exercise helps people in many ways. It strengthens the heart, improves sleep, and can even lift a person''s mood.','Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',NULL,'["Sleep matters more than exercise.","Regular exercise provides several health benefits.","Exercise is easy to start.","Hearts need rest more than movement."]',1,'The text lists multiple benefits of exercise.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-f3f835b8c065','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'The flowers in the vase ____ fresh and bright.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["has looked","looking","look","looks"]',2,'The plural subject ''flowers'' takes ''look''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-e363bd1299bc','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'Yesterday, the class ____ a trip to the science museum.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["taking","takes","will take","took"]',3,'''Yesterday'' requires the past tense.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-e708bba821f5','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'The storm knocked out the power ____ families lit candles and played board games.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'[",","; and",", and",": and"]',2,'A comma with a coordinating conjunction correctly joins two independent clauses.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-4f9a84d418ca','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'The ____ playground was closed for repairs.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["children","childrens''","childrens","children''s"]',3,'''Children'' is irregular plural, so the possessive is ''children''s''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-8e64f3327a6d','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Each of the students in the choir ____ a solo during the spring concert.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["perform","have performed","are performing","performs"]',3,'''Each'' is singular and takes ''performs''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-371e29d7a1bf','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The recipe requires three ingredients ____','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'[". Flour, butter, and sugar.",": flour, butter, and sugar."," flour, butter, and sugar.","; flour, butter, and sugar."]',1,'A colon after a complete clause introduces the list.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-4fbc7b69e8eb','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Walking along the shoreline, ____','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["there was a dolphin Marta saw leaping in the waves.","Marta spotted a dolphin leaping in the waves.","the waves held a leaping dolphin that Marta saw.","a dolphin was spotted by Marta leaping in the waves."]',1,'The opening modifier must describe Marta, the one walking.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-b00e856c6fe5','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The manager told the clerk that ____','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["their schedule had changed.","the clerk''s schedule had changed.","his schedule had changed.","her schedule had changed."]',1,'Repeating the noun avoids an ambiguous pronoun.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-32f1ac79a1a1','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'By the time the rescue team arrived, the hikers ____ for nearly six hours.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["are waiting","waited","have waited","had been waiting"]',3,'The past perfect progressive fits an action before a past event.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-cfcc5ad39fa3','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The lecture ran long ____ most of the audience stayed until the end.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["however,",": however","; however,",", however,"]',2,'A semicolon before a conjunctive adverb correctly joins independent clauses.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-1e772475d5bc','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Among the artifacts displayed in the new gallery ____ a bronze mirror and two ceramic bowls.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["was","has been","are","is"]',2,'The plural compound subject follows the verb and requires ''are''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-f74cf113b81c','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The mayor, who had promised to reduce traffic congestion ____ announced a new bus plan.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'[":","—",";",","]',3,'The nonrestrictive clause opened with a comma must close with a comma.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-567d8a1a0e5b','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The new program aims to reduce waste, conserve water, and ____ local farms.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["supports","has supported","supporting","support"]',3,'Parallel structure requires the base form ''support''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-7f9e646ba546','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'If the museum ____ more funding last year, it could have expanded its collection.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["would receive","had received","has received","received"]',1,'A past unreal conditional uses the past perfect.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-9be5f55ae1bc','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'The architect, ____ designed the library, will speak at tonight''s ceremony.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["which","she","whom","who"]',3,'''Who'' is the subject of ''designed'' and refers to a person.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-2d662f2a47fc','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'The award will go to the researcher ____ the committee believes made the most significant contribution.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["which","who","whose","whom"]',1,'''Who'' is the subject of ''made''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-325fb64382e1','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The company announced that ____ new headquarters would open in March.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["its","its''","it''s","they''re"]',0,'''Its'' is the singular possessive pronoun.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-2d4af438906c','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The festival, ____ draws thousands of visitors each year, features local musicians.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["it","and","that","which"]',3,'A nonrestrictive clause set off by commas needs ''which''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-f72df63d09e4','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'The author and her editor ____ revising the manuscript this week.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["was","are","has been","is"]',1,'A compound subject takes the plural ''are'' in the present.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-60b8e2b4936d','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'The two ____ essays were both selected for publication.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["students''s","students","students''","student''s"]',2,'The plural possessive of ''students'' is ''students''''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-44180fc2d162','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'As the sun set, the fishermen hauled in their nets, stacked the crates, and ____ toward the harbor.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["row","rowed","rowing","are rowing"]',1,'The past tense matches the other verbs in the series.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-1cb294953d0e','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'A tireless advocate for public libraries, ____','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["over two million dollars was raised by Naomi Fields''s campaign.","the campaign of Naomi Fields raised over two million dollars.","Naomi Fields raised over two million dollars through her campaign.","Naomi Fields''s campaign raised over two million dollars."]',2,'The modifier must be followed by the person it describes.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-56a3d823b9a1','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Books ____ are overdue incur a fine of ten cents per day.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["that",", which","who","what"]',0,'A restrictive clause about things uses ''that'' without commas.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-019e68938ea2','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Although the experiment failed ____ the researchers learned something valuable about the process.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'[";",",",":","."]',1,'A comma follows an introductory dependent clause.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-f3559ff28e5b','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'The two friends shared ____ lunch at the park.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["them","their","there","they''re"]',1,'''Their'' is the possessive pronoun.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-f2a91275d51c','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'The explorers lost most of ____ supplies in the river.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["its","their","there","they''re"]',1,'A plural noun takes the plural possessive ''their''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-735762a323b3','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The author ____ latest novel won the prize will tour next spring.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["which","whom","who''s","whose"]',3,'''Whose'' shows possession.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-f0e2c8af9d3d','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Neither the director nor the actors ____ satisfied with the first take.','Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?',NULL,'["were","has been","is","was"]',0,'The verb agrees with the nearer subject, ''actors''.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-68a438759fc5','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'The sky was dark and heavy with clouds. ____ the family decided to bring umbrellas on their walk.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Meanwhile,","Therefore,","However,","For example,"]',1,'Bringing umbrellas is a result of the dark clouds.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-37294322d5c4','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Solar panels have become far cheaper over the past decade. ____ many homeowners still hesitate to install them because of the upfront cost.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["In particular,","Even so,","As a result,","Likewise,"]',1,'The hesitation contrasts with the falling prices.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-b9280e326259','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Marine biologists tagged forty sharks off the coast. ____ they were able to track the animals'' migrations for more than two years.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["In contrast,","Instead,","Previously,","As a result,"]',3,'Tracking was a consequence of tagging.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-8ec4317719b9','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Some insects, such as ants, live in highly organized colonies. ____ other insects, such as most beetles, live largely solitary lives.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Consequently,","Similarly,","By contrast,","For instance,"]',2,'The sentences contrast social and solitary insects.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-74fe15240505','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'The author''s first novel sold few copies. ____ it earned a devoted following among critics, who later helped her second book reach a wide audience.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Likewise,","For example,","Therefore,","Still,"]',3,'Critical devotion contrasts with poor sales.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-876e9ec39b1e','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'Many plants rely on bees for pollination. ____ apple orchards often rent hives each spring to ensure a good harvest.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["On the other hand,","For this reason,","In other words,","Nonetheless,"]',1,'Orchards rent hives because of plants'' reliance on bees.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-ecb1f06064b5','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'The first draft of the bill proposed a flat tax. ____ the final version included three separate rates.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Specifically,","Ultimately,","Similarly,","For example,"]',1,'The final version came after the first draft.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-ecf1c050ebd4','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'Critics praised the film''s cinematography. ____ they found its screenplay uneven and its ending rushed.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Moreover,","Accordingly,","Likewise,","However,"]',3,'The second sentence contrasts with the praise.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-0fe280975030','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'Glass is made primarily from sand. ____ it is among the most easily recycled materials, since it can be melted and reformed without losing quality.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Instead,","Nevertheless,","Moreover,","Previously,"]',2,'The sentence adds another positive point about glass.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-0e0bbec90877','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'Early telescopes distorted images with colored fringes. ____ Newton''s reflecting telescope used a mirror instead of a lens, avoiding the problem altogether.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Likewise,","For example,","Accordingly,","In contrast,"]',3,'Newton''s design differs from the lens-based telescopes.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-d10097994372','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'The study found that students who slept eight hours scored higher on memory tests. ____ the researchers caution that the study cannot prove sleep caused the difference.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Similarly,","That said,","Therefore,","For example,"]',1,'The caution qualifies the finding.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-9621fb1a702a','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'The expedition''s maps proved inaccurate in several places. ____ the team reached the summit on schedule by relying on local guides'' knowledge.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Nonetheless,","Namely,","In addition,","Hence,"]',0,'Success despite faulty maps is a contrast.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-3319cfb57cbe','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'Sam loves to read. ____ he visits the library every Saturday.','Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?',NULL,'["Instead,","For example,","Otherwise,","However,"]',1,'The library visits illustrate his love of reading.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-eccf65adee20','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'- Kelp is a large brown algae.
- Kelp forests grow in cold, nutrient-rich coastal waters.
- A single kelp stalk can grow up to two feet in a day.
- Kelp forests shelter hundreds of marine species.','The student wants to emphasize how quickly kelp grows. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["Kelp, a large brown algae found in cold coastal waters, can grow as much as two feet in a single day.","Kelp forests shelter hundreds of marine species in cold coastal waters.","Kelp is a large brown algae that grows in nutrient-rich water.","Kelp stalks, which are found in forests, are large."]',0,'Only this choice highlights the daily growth rate.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-edec3bd744c4','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'- Tides of Ash (2015) is a historical novel set in Lisbon.
- Salt Road (2019) is a historical novel set in Morocco.
- Both were written by Ines Duarte.
- Both received national book awards.','The student wants to emphasize what the two books have in common. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["Tides of Ash was published in 2015, and Salt Road was published in 2019.","Ines Duarte wrote historical novels.","Ines Duarte wrote both Tides of Ash and Salt Road, historical novels that each received a national book award.","Tides of Ash is set in Lisbon, while Salt Road is set in Morocco."]',2,'This choice highlights shared features of both books.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-c84593eadb9a','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'- In 2021, divers found a shipwreck off Crete.
- The ship dates to roughly 400 B.C.E.
- Its cargo included over 300 intact wine jars.
- Residues in the jars let researchers study ancient winemaking.','The student wants to explain why the discovery matters to researchers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["The shipwreck found off Crete in 2021 matters because residues in its 300 intact wine jars let researchers study ancient winemaking.","The shipwreck, dating to roughly 400 B.C.E., carried over 300 intact wine jars.","Ancient winemaking was practiced on Crete.","Divers found a shipwreck off Crete in 2021."]',0,'Only this choice ties the find to its research value.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-eaff83824309','SAT','rw','mcq',1,'- The Maple Street Library opened in 1923.
- It has over 20,000 books.
- It hosts a story hour every Friday.','The student wants to briefly describe the library''s history. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["The library has over 20,000 books.","The Maple Street Library opened in 1923.","The library has many books and a story hour.","The library hosts a story hour every Friday."]',1,'Only the opening date concerns history.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-51af37893cca','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'- Researchers at Alder University studied 120 volunteers.
- Volunteers wore sleep trackers for 30 nights.
- Researchers compared sleep length with next-day reaction times.
- Longer sleep predicted faster reaction times.','The student wants to describe the study''s method. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["The study at Alder University showed that sleep matters.","Longer sleep predicted faster reaction times among volunteers.","Sleep trackers can measure how long people sleep.","Researchers at Alder University had 120 volunteers wear sleep trackers for 30 nights and compared their sleep length with next-day reaction times."]',3,'Only this choice describes how the study was conducted.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-e22c3ded1cc7','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'- Electric buses cost about 40 percent more than diesel buses to buy.
- Electric buses cost far less to fuel and maintain.
- Over twelve years, total costs for electric buses were about 15 percent lower in one city''s analysis.','The student wants to argue that electric buses are cost-effective despite a higher purchase price. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["Electric buses cost far less to fuel and maintain than diesel buses.","Although electric buses cost about 40 percent more to buy, lower fuel and maintenance costs made their total cost about 15 percent lower over twelve years in one city''s analysis.","One city analyzed electric buses over twelve years.","Electric buses cost about 40 percent more than diesel buses to buy."]',1,'This choice acknowledges the price and then shows overall savings.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-3e9f93f2bbd6','SAT','rw','mcq',4,'- Wangari Pierce is a ceramic artist.
- She grew up in Nairobi and now lives in Toronto.
- Her work combines Kenyan basket patterns with glazed stoneware.
- Her first solo show opens in May.','The student wants to explain what is distinctive about the artist''s work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["Wangari Pierce, who grew up in Nairobi, now lives in Toronto.","Ceramic artist Wangari Pierce combines Kenyan basket patterns with glazed stoneware.","Wangari Pierce''s first solo show opens in May.","Wangari Pierce is a ceramic artist with an upcoming show."]',1,'Only this choice describes her style.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-4ea57b79cafa','SAT','rw','mcq',2,'- Fern Trail is 3 miles round trip.
- The trail is mostly flat.
- It passes two waterfalls.
- Parking is free.','The student wants to suggest the hike to inexperienced hikers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["Fern Trail is 3 miles long.","Parking at Fern Trail is free.","Fern Trail passes two waterfalls and has free parking.","Fern Trail, a mostly flat 3-mile round trip, is well suited to inexperienced hikers and passes two waterfalls."]',3,'Flat terrain and short length are the details relevant to beginners.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-37a553db3639','SAT','rw','mcq',5,'- In 1950, about 60 percent of town residents farmed.
- By 2000, about 5 percent did.
- Over that period, the town''s population doubled.
- Most new residents worked in manufacturing.','The student wants to emphasize how sharply farming''s share of the town''s work changed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["The share of residents who farmed fell from about 60 percent in 1950 to about 5 percent in 2000, even as the town''s population doubled.","The town''s population doubled between 1950 and 2000.","About 5 percent of residents farmed in 2000.","Most new residents of the town worked in manufacturing."]',0,'This choice gives both endpoints of the change.',1,0);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id,exam_type,section,kind,difficulty,passage,stem,image_svg,choices,answer,explanation,active,created_at) VALUES ('seed-sat-rw-880234dbcdbc','SAT','rw','mcq',3,'- The axolotl is a salamander native to Mexico.
- Most salamanders lose their gills as adults.
- Axolotls keep their gills for life.
- Axolotls can regrow lost limbs.','The student wants to explain how the axolotl differs from most other salamanders. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish the stated goal?',NULL,'["Axolotls are salamanders that can regrow lost limbs.","The axolotl is a salamander native to Mexico.","Most salamanders are not native to Mexico.","Unlike most salamanders, which lose their gills as adults, the axolotl keeps its gills for life."]',3,'This choice sets the axolotl against most salamanders.',1,0);
